 ],
 "literal": "The sun having set in the wide-expanse sky, / pitiable indeed are the curved-winged birds! / On the lofty kadamba tree beside the path where they perch and dwell, / to feed inside the mouths of their chicks, / having obtained food, they indeed hasten their flight.",
 "translation": "The sun has set in the wide expanse of the sky. How pitiable are the curved-winged birds! Having gathered food to feed inside the mouths of their chicks waiting in their high nests on the kadamba tree beside the path, they hasten their homeward flight.",
 "modern": "சூரியன் மறைந்த அகன்ற வானத்தில், வளைந்த சிறகுகளைக் கொண்ட பறவைகள் இரக்கத்திற்குரியவை. வழியின் பக்கத்தில் உள்ள உயர்ந்த மராஅ மரத்தில் இருக்கும் தங்களுடைய கூடுகளில் உள்ள குஞ்சுகளின் வாயினுள் ஊட்டுவதற்காக, இரையைத் தேடிக் கொண்டுவிட்டதால், அவை தங்களுடைய இருப்பிடத்தை நோக்கி விரைந்து பறக்கின்றன.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the heroine to her companion or to herself during the evening, expressing her deep grief over the hero's absence. As the sun sets, she observes the curved-winged birds returning to their nests on the tall kadamba tree beside the path. Having gathered food, these birds are rushing back to feed their hungry chicks waiting in the nests. The heroine feels that even these simple birds show deep love and responsibility by returning home at dusk, whereas her beloved hero remains away. This contrast between the birds' timely return and the hero's delay intensifies her loneliness and longing.",
